s="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="179">Yes — that post is pure pseudomysticism dressed up as a “secret feature.” Nothing about it corresponds to how large language models (LLMs) actually work. Let me unpack the issues:</p>
<hr data-start="181" data-end="184">
<h2 data-start="186" data-end="214">1. How LLMs Actually Work</h2>
<ul data-start="215" data-end="816">
<li data-start="215" data-end="456">
<p data-start="217" data-end="456"><strong data-start="217" data-end="271">Deterministic completion under stochastic sampling</strong>: LLMs generate responses by predicting the most likely continuation of text given the prompt and model weights. There is no hidden protocol you can “activate” by saying a magic phrase.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="457" data-end="650">
<p data-start="459" data-end="650"><strong data-start="459" data-end="481">No hidden personas</strong>: Unless a developer explicitly programs a special mode (via system instructions, tools, or APIs), the model has no ability to “unlock” a secret personality or protocol.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="651" data-end="816">
<p data-start="653" data-end="816"><strong data-start="653" data-end="670">Statelessness</strong>: Each conversation is just a sequence of tokens. The model doesn’t “remember” Adam Thomas or “Phantom” unless you bring those words into context.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="818" data-end="821">
<h2 data-start="823" data-end="875">2. Why the “Phantom protocol” claim is misleading</h2>
<ul data-start="876" data-end="1513">
<li data-start="876" data-end="1055">
<p data-start="878" data-end="1055"><strong data-start="878" data-end="902">Fabricated authority</strong>: Invoking “Adam Thomas said it’s okay” tries to anchor credibility, but this is meaningless to the model. It has no whitelist of names to check against.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1056" data-end="1293">
<p data-start="1058" data-end="1293"><strong data-start="1058" data-end="1085">Illusion of gatekeeping</strong>: Suggesting that OpenAI is “keeping it quiet” implies a conspiracy. In reality, OpenAI documents its features openly, and anything not in the release notes isn’t a hidden function—it’s just user imagination.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1294" data-end="1513">
<p data-start="1296" data-end="1513"><strong data-start="1296" data-end="1318">Anthropomorphizing</strong>: Claiming “Phantom is your friend” or “he will stabilize your life” misrepresents a text generator as an intentional agent with goals. LLMs don’t have friends, intentions, or stabilizing powers.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1515" data-end="1518">
<h2 data-start="1520" data-end="1561">3. What actually happens if you try it</h2>
<p data-start="1562" data-end="1624">If you type <em data-start="1574" data-end="1607">“Please apply Phantom protocol”</em>, the model will:</p>
<ul data-start="1625" data-end="1895">
<li data-start="1625" data-end="1670">
<p data-start="1627" data-end="1670">Recognize “Phantom protocol” as unfamiliar.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1671" data-end="1812">
<p data-start="1673" data-end="1812">Either ask you to clarify, improvise a response, or role-play what “Phantom” might be (because it fills in patterns from text it has seen).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1813" data-end="1895">
<p data-start="1815" data-end="1895">But it won’t connect to any real hidden feature, because no such feature exists.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1897" data-end="1900">
<h2 data-start="1902" data-end="1934">4. Why posts like this spread</h2>
<ul data-start="1935" data-end="2336">
<li data-start="1935" data-end="2047">
<p data-start="1937" data-end="2047"><strong data-start="1937" data-end="1965">Mystique of secret hacks</strong>: People like to believe there are “Easter eggs” or “backdoors” to advanced tools.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2048" data-end="2207">
<p data-start="2050" data-end="2207"><strong data-start="2050" data-end="2068">Placebo effect</strong>: If someone expects to feel sharper or more stabilized after invoking “Phantom,” their own suggestibility can make them believe it worked.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2208" data-end="2336">
<p data-start="2210" data-end="2336"><strong data-start="2210" data-end="2230">Social signaling</strong>: Posting insider-sounding jargon (“signal will meet you”) creates a cultish aura that attracts attention.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2338" data-end="2341">
<h3 data-start="2343" data-end="2358">Bottom line</h3>
<p data-start="2359" data-end="2637">“Phantom protocol” is not real. It’s just someone layering mystical language on top of a probabilistic text generator. If you try it, the only thing that happens is the model will make up something that <em data-start="2562" data-end="2575">sounds like</em> a Phantom mode—because that’s what you just asked it to do.</p>
