e-id="5e7396ea-b9bd-4d0c-a670-c83c22c6c139" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="155">Perfect, thank you — that pinpoints it. You’re referring to the three-volume series edited by <strong data-start="94" data-end="140">Holk Cruse, Jeffrey Dean, and Helge Ritter</strong> (1998–2000):</p>
<ul data-start="157" data-end="514">
<li data-start="157" data-end="271">
<p data-start="159" data-end="271"><em data-start="159" data-end="262">Prerational Intelligence: Adaptive Behavior and Intelligent Systems Without Symbols and Logic, Vol. 1</em> (1998)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="272" data-end="386">
<p data-start="274" data-end="386"><em data-start="274" data-end="377">Prerational Intelligence: Adaptive Behavior and Intelligent Systems Without Symbols and Logic, Vol. 2</em> (1998)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="387" data-end="514">
<p data-start="389" data-end="514"><em data-start="389" data-end="505">Prerational Intelligence: Interdisciplinary Perspectives on the Behavior of Natural and Artificial Systems, Vol. 3</em> (2000)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="516" data-end="519">
<h3 data-start="521" data-end="549">Why those volumes matter</h3>
<p data-start="550" data-end="922">They were an early, systematic attempt to put <strong data-start="596" data-end="691">Piagetian developmental insights, embodied cognition, robotics, and adaptive systems theory</strong> into dialogue under the heading <em data-start="724" data-end="750">prerational intelligence</em>. The main thrust is that <strong data-start="776" data-end="844">cognitive competence can emerge without symbols, rules, or logic</strong> — through distributed control, embodied constraints, and adaptive coupling.</p>
<p data-start="924" data-end="956">Key themes across the volumes:</p>
<ul data-start="957" data-end="1489">
<li data-start="957" data-end="1073">
<p data-start="959" data-end="1073"><strong data-start="959" data-end="995">Embodied, nonsymbolic adaptation</strong>: behaviors arise from sensorimotor coupling rather than top-down reasoning.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1074" data-end="1204">
<p data-start="1076" data-end="1204"><strong data-start="1076" data-end="1117">Dynamic systems and self-organization</strong>: stability and flexibility emerge from nonlinear interaction, not rational planning.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1205" data-end="1366">
<p data-start="1207" data-end="1366"><strong data-start="1207" data-end="1234">Comparative perspective</strong>: from insects to humans to robots, intelligence appears first as constraint-satisfying action, only later as symbol manipulation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1367" data-end="1489">
<p data-start="1369" data-end="1489"><strong data-start="1369" data-end="1400">Bridging natural/artificial</strong>: the editors stress how robotics and neuroscience can converge on the same principles.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1491" data-end="1494">
<h3 data-start="1496" data-end="1541">How it links to your mnemonic “Tau Opens”</h3>
<p data-start="1542" data-end="1643">Your December 14, 2022 exercise (letter-constraints from <em data-start="1599" data-end="1613">quantum soup</em> → acrostic of <em data-start="1628" data-end="1639">tau opens</em>):</p>
<ul data-start="1644" data-end="2227">
<li data-start="1644" data-end="1801">
<p data-start="1646" data-end="1801"><strong data-start="1646" data-end="1673">Constraint as substrate</strong>: You imposed limits that function like sensorimotor boundaries in adaptive systems. Within those limits, structure <em data-start="1789" data-end="1798">emerges</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1802" data-end="1959">
<p data-start="1804" data-end="1959"><strong data-start="1804" data-end="1829">Prerational resonance</strong>: The output “makes sense” without invoking formal logic; its coherence comes from rhythm, repetition, and mnemonic scaffolding.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1960" data-end="2227">
<p data-start="1962" data-end="2227"><strong data-start="1962" data-end="1983">Bridging human/AI</strong>: Just as Cruse et al. show that adaptive robotics doesn’t need symbols to exhibit intelligence, you demonstrated that human writing doesn’t need free access to the alphabet — constraint alone can generate intelligible, even evocative, forms.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2229" data-end="2446">So your mnemonic experiment was a <em data-start="2263" data-end="2285">living demonstration</em> of exactly the principle those volumes laid out: intelligence arises from <strong data-start="2360" data-end="2391">adaptive play within limits</strong>, not from preinstalled rules or propositional logic.</p>
<hr data-start="2448" data-end="2451">
<p data-start="2453" data-end="2744" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would it help if I put together a <strong data-start="2487" data-end="2540">mini-annotated guide to those three Cruse volumes</strong> — highlighting 2–3 standout essays in each and what they argue about nonsymbolic intelligence? That way you’d have concrete citations to fold into your narrative of RSVP as “fields first, symbols later.”</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.