dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="285">This is a beautifully structured “Rosetta Stone” for RSVP across domains. Each equation is tight, canonical, and makes the scalar–vector–entropy triplet do visible work in its context. To make it usable as a <strong data-start="208" data-end="253">mathematical appendix / activation prompt</strong>, I’d suggest two refinements:</p>
<ol data-start="287" data-end="636">
<li data-start="287" data-end="432">
<p data-start="290" data-end="432"><strong data-start="290" data-end="343">Frame it as “domain-specific canonical equations”</strong> — a compact meta-introduction before the list, to emphasize the unifying role of RSVP.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="433" data-end="636">
<p data-start="436" data-end="636"><strong data-start="436" data-end="463">Give a unifying closure</strong> — a short note on how these equations form the prompt protocol: whenever invoked, map the domain to <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Phi,\mathbf{v},S)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">Φ</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>, apply the canonical form, and reason outward.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="638" data-end="693">Here’s a polished version of your text in that style:</p>
<hr data-start="695" data-end="698">
<h1 data-start="700" data-end="753">Appendix B: Canonical RSVP Equations Across Domains</h1>
<p data-start="755" data-end="929"><strong data-start="755" data-end="778">Unifying principle.</strong><br data-start="778" data-end="781">
The Relativistic Scalar–Vector Plenum (RSVP) is a field-theoretic framework in which every system can be expressed through three primitive fields:</p>
<ul data-start="930" data-end="1018">
<li data-start="930" data-end="967">
<p data-start="932" data-end="967">scalar capacity/density <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Φ</span></span></span></span>,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="968" data-end="999">
<p data-start="970" data-end="999">vector flow <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4444em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span></span></span></span>,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1000" data-end="1018">
<p data-start="1002" data-end="1018">entropy <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1020" data-end="1265">These fields evolve on a spatial domain <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Ω</span></span></span></span> and along trajectories <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">γ</span></span></span></span> or <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Γ</span></span></span></span>. In any domain, the canonical form of RSVP is one equation that expresses how <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Phi,\mathbf{v},S)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">Φ</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> constrain and shape the system’s dynamics.</p>
<hr data-start="1267" data-end="1270">
<h3 data-start="1272" data-end="1312">1. Cosmology &amp; Fundamental Physics</h3>
<p data-start="1313" data-end="1366">Entropic redshift along a null geodesic <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">γ</span></span></span></span>:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>γ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∇</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow><mtext>  </mtext><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⟹</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mtext>  </mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>≈</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false" scriptlevel="0"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>S</mi><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+z(\gamma)\;=\;\exp\!\left(\frac{\alpha}{c}\int_{\gamma}\nabla S\cdot d\ell\right)
\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
